  "summary": "The day brings significant developments across pharma, regulatory policy, and health-tech infrastructure. On the regulatory front, Congress is pushing back against plans to create a political appointee panel to review NIH grants, while Grail's blood cancer detection test is advancing through FDA advisory committee review. The pharma sector shows continued momentum with Lilly's approval of a new breast cancer combination therapy, Alkermes' early-stage ADHD results, and Novo Nordisk outlining expansion plans for its obesity franchise and direct-to-consumer initiatives. In the investment and M&A space, radiopharmaceutical dealmaking continues with Telix acquiring ITM Isotope Technologies for $1.65 billion, while Oura is targeting a $2.2 billion IPO and Electra raised $350 million for precision immunology drugs. Health-tech priorities are shifting toward AI integration in clinical workflows—with ARPA-H funding seven organizations for an AI cardiovascular care platform and multiple stories emphasizing the need to involve clinicians in AI implementation. Additional areas gaining attention include environmental sustainability in healthcare delivery, the role of wearables, and workforce issues around nursing shortages, alongside emerging public health concerns like rising kratom and 7-OH withdrawal cases in addiction clinics.",
